Last week I [replaced Lunr.js with Pagefind](/posts/20260228-replacing-lunr-with-pagefind/) for keyword search on this site. Pagefind works well -- you type "eleventy image plugin" and get back pages containing those words, ranked by [BM25](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okapi_BM25). But keyword search has a ceiling. Search for "making websites faster" and you'll miss a post titled "performance optimization" because the words don't overlap.

With LLM-powered search showing up everywhere, I wanted to see how close I could get to that natural-language experience on a static site without any cloud infrastructure. No API keys, no SaaS, no server. Could the same embedding model run at build time and in the browser, and would the results actually be useful?

The answer is mostly yes. You have to download ~30 MB on first use, which is a lot. But everything caches, it runs entirely on your device, and the results are surprisingly good -- it finds posts by meaning, not just matching words.

To be honest, this is a bit silly. I don't get much search traffic, and Pagefind already handles keyword search well. But this site is my playground -- the place where I try things to see how they work, then think about where they might matter at a bigger scale. Vector search on a personal blog is a toy. Vector search on a documentation site with hundreds of pages and users who don't know the right terminology? That's a different conversation.

> ## tl;dr
>
> - Build-time script generates vector embeddings for all posts using [MiniLM-L6-v2](https://huggingface.co/Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2) via [Transformers.js](https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js)
> - Produces a ~700 KB `vectors.json` file shipped as a static asset
> - Browser loads the same model on first query (~30 MB with WASM runtime, cached afterward)
> - Cosine similarity ranks posts by meaning, not keywords
> - [Try it on the search page](/search/?search_type=semantic)

## How vector search works

Traditional search (Lunr, Pagefind, Elasticsearch) builds an inverted index: for each word, store which documents contain it. Query terms get looked up in the index and documents are ranked by term frequency, document length, and similar signals. This works well when the user's words match the author's words.

Vector search takes a different approach. A small neural network (in this case, [all-MiniLM-L6-v2](https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2)) reads a piece of text and produces a fixed-length array of numbers -- a 384-dimension vector -- that captures the text's meaning. Two texts about similar topics produce vectors that point in similar directions, even if they share no words.

At query time, the same model converts the question into a vector. [Cosine similarity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosine_similarity) measures how close the query vector is to each document vector. The closest documents are the most relevant results.

The entire ranking function is ten lines:

```js
function cosineSimilarity(a, b) {
  let dot = 0, normA = 0, normB = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    dot += a[i] * b[i];
    normA += a[i] * a[i];
    normB += b[i] * b[i];
  }
  return dot / (Math.sqrt(normA) * Math.sqrt(normB));
}
```

## The build-time half

A Node.js script runs after Eleventy and Pagefind in the build pipeline. It reads every HTML file in the `build/` directory, extracts text from `<main data-pagefind-body>` (reusing Pagefind's content boundary), and generates an embedding for each page.

The input to the model is the page title, meta description, and the first 500 characters of body text concatenated together. More on why that specific combination in the next section.

```js
const text = title + ' ' + description + ' ' + bodyText.slice(0, 500);
const output = await extractor(text, { pooling: 'mean', normalize: true });
```

The output is a JSON file:

```json
{
  "model": "Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2",
  "dimension": 384,
  "documents": [
    {
      "url": "/posts/example/",
      "title": "...",
      "teaser": "...",
      "date": "2026-02-28",
      "embedding": [0.023, -0.041, ...]
    }
  ]
}
```

For 88 pages, the file is 719 KB raw. Gzip brings that down to roughly 80 KB over the wire. The embedding step adds about six seconds to a production build (after the model is cached locally).

The `package.json` change is minimal:

```json
"embeddings": "node scripts/generate-embeddings.js",
"build": "yarn clean && yarn sass && yarn eleventy && yarn embeddings && echo 'Build completed'"
```

Embeddings run after Eleventy (which runs Pagefind in its `eleventy.after` hook), so the full HTML output is available.

The `@huggingface/transformers` dependency is heavy -- roughly 476 MB in `node_modules`, mostly [ONNX Runtime](https://onnxruntime.ai/) native binaries for running the model in Node.js. It ships prebuilt binaries for every platform (macOS, Linux, Windows, multiple architectures), and there's no way to install only the one you need. This is the same kind of tradeoff as Pagefind shipping a Rust binary for its indexing step: the build-time tooling is large because something has to actually run the neural network over your content. The output is small and fast to query, but producing it requires real compute. A Python script with just [`onnxruntime`](https://pypi.org/project/onnxruntime/) and [`tokenizers`](https://pypi.org/project/tokenizers/) (no PyTorch) would cut the footprint to ~75-100 MB -- [ChromaDB uses this approach](https://github.com/chroma-core/onnx-embedding) in production. You could also compile a Rust binary with [fastembed-rs](https://github.com/Anush008/fastembed-rs), though it's a library, not a CLI, so you'd need to write a wrapper. Either way, it means adding a non-Node dependency to the build. For now, one fat npm install is simpler.

The embedding script is not included in `yarn dev` -- it's too slow for iterative development. Run `yarn build` once to generate vectors, then `yarn dev` as usual. The model files for the browser (more on that below) are downloaded separately in an `eleventy.after` hook, so `yarn dev` still has them even without running the full build pipeline.

## The browser half

The semantic search UI loads nothing on initial visit. On first query submit, three things happen:

1. Fetch `/semantic-search/vectors.json` (~80 KB gzipped)
2. Import Transformers.js from [jsDelivr](https://www.jsdelivr.com/) CDN
3. Initialize the MiniLM pipeline (~23 MB model + ~4 MB WASM runtime, with progress bar)

The model and runtime download once and cache normally. Subsequent queries within the same session are near-instant -- embed the query (~100 ms), compute 88 cosine similarities (< 1 ms), sort and display.

```js
// Lazy-load on first query, not on page load
const { env, pipeline } = await import('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@huggingface/transformers@3');
env.allowLocalModels = true;
env.localModelPath = '/';

extractor = await pipeline('feature-extraction', 'semantic-search/model', {
  dtype: 'q8',
  progress_callback: (progress) => {
    if (progress.status === 'progress') {
      progressBar.value = Math.round(progress.progress);
    }
  },
});
```

### Self-hosting the model

The model files are self-hosted rather than loaded from HuggingFace at query time. HuggingFace serves large files through signed CloudFront URLs -- the download URL changes on every request, so the browser cache never matches and the 23 MB model re-downloads on every visit.

An `eleventy.after` hook downloads the four model files (config, tokenizer, tokenizer config, quantized ONNX weights) from HuggingFace once and deploys them as static assets at `build/semantic-search/model/`. The hook skips the download if the files already exist, so dev rebuilds stay fast. The browser loads them from the same origin with normal HTTP caching.

Getting Transformers.js to load from your own server instead of HuggingFace takes some coaxing -- that's what the `env.allowLocalModels` and `env.localModelPath` lines in the code above are doing. Transformers.js disables "local" model loading in browsers by default, and it validates model identifiers against a HuggingFace regex, so bare paths like `/semantic-search/model/` and full URLs both fail. The model ID has to look like `org/model` -- `semantic-search/model` passes the check, and with `localModelPath` set to `/`, the library fetches `/semantic-search/model/tokenizer.json` and so on. Works the same in dev and production.

I thought about self-hosting the Transformers.js library and ONNX Runtime WASM engine too, but jsDelivr uses deterministic URLs that cache normally. The signed-URL problem is specific to HuggingFace's model hosting. Since this page is more playground than critical infrastructure, the CDN dependency is fine.

To update the model, change `MODEL_NAME` in `scripts/generate-embeddings.js` and `modelName` in `eleventy.js`, then rebuild. Both sides need the same model -- build-time vectors and browser-side query vectors have to come from the same embedding space.

Results are filtered by a similarity threshold (score > 0.25) and limited to five. Below that threshold, results tend to be noise. The response templates are simple conditionals -- no LLM generates the answer text:

- Zero results: "I couldn't find anything closely related. Try rephrasing, or use keyword search."
- One high-confidence result: "I found one post that closely matches:"
- Multiple results: "Here are N posts that might be relevant:"

Every response includes a fallback link to Pagefind keyword search.

## What the model sees

MiniLM-L6-v2 is a [sentence transformer](https://www.sbert.net/) -- a BERT variant fine-tuned for producing sentence embeddings. It was trained on [over a billion sentence pairs](https://huggingface.co/sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2) and distilled from a larger model. The "L6" means six transformer layers (BERT-base has twelve), and the output is 384 dimensions (half of BERT's 768). Smaller and faster, at the cost of some nuance.

It doesn't understand content the way a large language model would. It maps text into a geometric space where similar meanings cluster together. "Performance optimization" and "making websites faster" land near each other because the model learned from millions of examples that those phrases appear in similar contexts.

The input you feed it matters more than I expected. Just the title loses too much nuance. The entire article dilutes the signal with boilerplate. Title + description + first 500 characters of body text turned out to be a good balance -- enough to capture the topic, short enough that the embedding stays focused.

## Pagefind and semantic search compared

| | Pagefind (keyword) | Semantic search |
|---|---|---|
| Query type | Exact and fuzzy keyword matching | Natural language, meaning-based |
| Initial download | ~10 KB JS + ~75 KB WASM | ~80 KB vectors + ~30 MB model + runtime (cached) |
| Per-query cost | ~10-30 KB index fragments | ~100 ms compute, no network |
| Best for | Known terms, specific phrases | Exploratory questions, topic discovery |
| Excerpts | Yes, highlighted in context | No (shows teaser only) |
| Build dependency | Rust binary (via npm) | ~476 MB ONNX runtime (via npm) |

They solve different problems. Pagefind is better when you know the words. Semantic search is better when you know the idea but not the vocabulary. Both live on the same search page -- keyword search is the default, and semantic search is an opt-in that loads on demand.

A few examples from this site to make it concrete.

Search for "design systems" on Pagefind and you get the one post with those words in the title. Semantic search also finds posts about decoupling content from platforms, building reusable components for scientific websites, and untangling an organization's navigation -- related work that never uses the phrase "design system."

Try "how to work with AI effectively" and Pagefind matches everything with "AI" in it, which is half the blog at this point. Semantic search ranks the context engineering and integration posts highest and pushes the less relevant mentions further down.

"Measuring website success" is another good one. Pagefind finds posts with "measuring" in the title. Semantic search connects it to the analytics infrastructure post about audience quality and to a post about doubling conversion rates -- related by concept, not vocabulary.

The semantic results aren't always right. Some matches feel like a stretch, and it misses things. But the queries where it works well are exactly the ones where keyword search falls flat.

## Tradeoffs

**The 30 MB elephant.** The first query downloads the quantized model (~23 MB) plus the ONNX Runtime WASM engine (~4 MB), tokenizer, and Transformers.js library -- about 30 MB total. The full-precision model is 90 MB; 8-bit quantization (`dtype: 'q8'`) gets it to 23 MB with negligible quality loss for sentence embeddings. Everything caches, so repeat visitors pay nothing, but the first query on a new device is a real wait. The progress bar helps set expectations. I looked at lighter alternatives (see the Model2Vec note in "What I'd change") but nothing I tested matched the result quality of running a real transformer -- the 30 MB is the cost of results that actually work.

**No excerpts.** Pagefind shows highlighted excerpts with the matching terms in context. Semantic search can only show the teaser from frontmatter. The model doesn't know which sentences were most relevant to the query, just that the overall document is similar. Adding extractive summarization would help but is a much harder problem.

**Threshold tuning.** The 0.25 cosine similarity cutoff is hand-tuned. Too low and irrelevant results creep in. Too high and you miss valid matches. There's no perfect number -- it depends on the corpus and the kinds of queries people ask.

**CDN dependency.** Transformers.js and the ONNX Runtime WASM engine load from jsDelivr. The model files are self-hosted (HuggingFace's signed URLs break browser caching), but the JS and WASM cache fine from jsDelivr. If jsDelivr is down, the page shows an error with a link to keyword search.

**Build-time coupling.** The embedding script reads Eleventy's HTML output directly, so it depends on the build directory structure. If the HTML format changes, the text extraction regex could break. A proper HTML parser would be more robust, but regex handles the current templates fine.

**No conversational LLM.** You could add a small LLM on top of the embeddings to generate natural-language answers -- "Based on these three posts, you probably want..." -- instead of a plain ranked list. I skipped it. Nobody uses the search page to chat; they want to find a post. A list with teasers and match scores does that.

The size gap is also hard to justify. Even small LLMs (1-3B parameters) need 1-4 GB downloads and [WebGPU support](https://caniuse.com/webgpu), which still isn't in Firefox or on older devices. Embeddings alone are ~30 MB and run on WASM. If conversational models shrink to the point where they run on CPU without a multi-gigabyte download, I'd reconsider.

## Tips if you're doing this

1. **Reuse your existing content boundary.** If you already have `data-pagefind-body` or similar markup, use it as the extraction boundary for embeddings too. One source of truth for "what counts as content."

2. **Concatenate title + description + truncated body.** Don't feed the model raw HTML or entire articles. The title and description carry disproportionate signal. A few hundred characters of body text adds topic specificity without diluting the embedding.

3. **Lazy-load everything.** Don't download the model on page load. Most visitors will never use semantic search. Load on first interaction and show a progress bar.

4. **Ship both search types.** Keyword and semantic search solve different problems. Cross-link between them so users can switch when one doesn't find what they need.

5. **Set a similarity threshold.** Without a cutoff, every query returns results -- even nonsense queries. Start at 0.25 and adjust based on your corpus.

6. **Self-host the model files.** HuggingFace serves ONNX files through signed CloudFront URLs that change on every request, breaking browser cache. Download them at build time and serve as static assets. You'll need to set `env.allowLocalModels = true` and `env.localModelPath` to your site root, and use a model ID in `org/model` format so it passes Transformers.js's validation.

## What I'd change

**Show snippets, not just teasers.** Right now the results show the frontmatter teaser for each post. Embedding individual paragraphs or sections instead of whole documents would let me show which *part* of a post matches the query. Storage grows -- maybe 10-15x more embeddings -- but the results would be much more useful.

**Shrink the vectors.** Each embedding is 384 float32 values (1,536 bytes). [Binary quantization](https://huggingface.co/blog/embedding-quantization) thresholds each value to a single bit and uses Hamming distance instead of cosine similarity. That's a 32x size reduction -- the vectors in vectors.json would go from ~700 KB to ~4 KB. Models trained with [Matryoshka representation learning](https://huggingface.co/blog/matryoshka) (like [mxbai-embed-xsmall-v1](https://huggingface.co/mixedbread-ai/mxbai-embed-xsmall-v1)) let you also truncate dimensions -- 384 down to 128 with ~85% quality retained. Combine both and each embedding becomes 16 bytes. For 88 documents this barely matters, but for thousands of chunks it would.

**Eliminate the ONNX runtime with static embeddings.** [Model2Vec](https://github.com/MinishLab/model2vec) distills a transformer into a lookup table -- tokenize, index into a matrix, average, normalize. No neural network inference, no WASM runtime. The browser download would drop from ~30 MB to ~4-15 MB and query-time inference would be instant. I tested the [potion](https://huggingface.co/minishlab) models (2M, 4M, and 8M parameters) against MiniLM-L6-v2 on my actual content, though, and the results were disappointing. For queries like "improving search on a static site," MiniLM correctly finds the two search-related posts. All three potion models miss both entirely. On "design systems," potion returns the 404 page, Privacy Policy, and Colophon -- pages with short generic text that become false attractors after mean pooling. The Jaccard overlap between MiniLM's top results and potion-8M's was about 32% across my test queries. On [MTEB benchmarks](https://huggingface.co/blog/Pringled/model2vec), potion-base-8M scores ~89% of MiniLM's average, but that gap matters more on a small corpus where the margin between a good result and garbage is thin. If static embedding models keep improving -- and they are -- this could change fast.

**Merge keyword and semantic results more deeply.** Keyword and semantic search now live on the same page -- semantic is an opt-in below keyword results. The next step would be to load the embedding model in a background Web Worker and merge the two result sets automatically when the model is ready. The user always sees keyword results immediately instead of watching a progress bar, and semantic results appear alongside when ready. [Sift](https://github.com/romanI04/sift) does exactly this -- it stores FTS5 keyword indexes and vector embeddings in a single SQLite file, shows keyword results first, then upgrades to semantic. It also uses HTTP range requests to read only the index pages it needs, which would matter for larger sites.

For now, it's two search modes on one page on a static site with no backend -- overkill for a personal blog, but a useful sketch for how this could work on sites where search actually matters. [Try it](/search/?search_type=semantic).

